%S A399068 8,20,31,32,43,54,260,272,283,512,3129,3141,3152,3381,6250,46660,
%T A399068 46672,46683,46912,49781,65540,65552,65563,65792,68661,93312,112192,
%U A399068 131072,823547,823559,823570,823799,826668,870199,889079,1647086,16777220,16777232,16777243
%N A399068 Numbers of the form x^^y + w^^z, where x, y, w, z are integers greater than 1 and ^^ denotes tetration.
%C A399068 All the given terms of the present sequence (i.e., a(1) to a(39)) are certified, since both summands are positive: an exhaustive search over all tetrations up to 16777243 is sufficient to determine exactly all terms not exceeding a(39).
%C A399068 Consecutive terms can be used to bracket real numbers between sums of two integer tetrations, giving lower and upper approximations in terms of power towers (e.g., a(2) = 2^^2 + 2^^3 < e^3 < 2^^2 + 3^^2 = a(3)).
%H A399068 MathOverflow, <a href="https://mathoverflow.net/questions/514311/tetrational-analogue-of-catalans-conjecture-is-2-3-3-2-11-the-smallest">Tetrational analogue of Catalan's conjecture: is 2^^3 - 3^^2 = 11?</a>.
%e A399068 a(1) = 2^^2 + 2^^2.
%e A399068 a(2) = 2^^2 + 2^^3.
%e A399068 a(3) = 2^^2 + 3^^2.
%e A399068 a(4) = 2^^3 + 2^^3.
%e A399068 a(5) = 2^^3 + 3^^2.
%Y A399068 Cf. A275211, A321312, A364270, A393643.
